  ],
  "text": "For all of human history, every sound ever made was gone instantly. That ended in eighteen seventy seven. Speech, music, a voice you loved. All of it vanished the moment it was made. The phonograph did something almost absurdly direct. It let the sound do the carving. The air shakes a membrane, the membrane shakes a needle, and the needle scratches foil. A vinyl groove is not a code for the wave. It is the wave, cut into a spiral. Tape did the same thing in magnetism instead of grooves. Then somebody stopped copying the shape and started measuring it instead. Measure the height thousands of times a second and the sound becomes a list of numbers. And a number can be copied perfectly. A groove wears out. A count does not. The moment sound became numbers, it could go anywhere. That is the whole modern media economy. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
